  "response": "**VERDICT: REJECT**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component implements the structural skeleton of a WAI-ARIA Menu Button / Listbox pattern correctly (using `<button>` and `role=\"listbox\"`), but fails on two critical interaction design decisions: focus restoration and roving tabindex. Keyboard users are stranded on the page body after making a selection or pressing Escape, and screen reader users cannot perceive which option is highlighted because DOM focus never moves to the options. These are not minor polish issues; they break the core interaction model for assistive technology users.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**: \nBefore reading code, I predicted:\n1. Focus management after closing (Escape/selection) would be missing or accidental.\n2. Roving tabindex would be incomplete (state updates without DOM focus movement).\n3. `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls` would be present but potentially mismatched to the trigger element.\n4. The component might rely on visual-only selection indicators for screen readers.\n\nActual findings confirmed predictions 1 and 2 as critical design gaps. Predictions 3 and 4 were not borne out (ARIA attributes are correctly wired), but the missing focus management renders them functionally inert for keyboard/AT users.\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\n1. **Focus restoration to trigger is missing on close.**\n   - **Evidence**: `Component Code:17` (`handleToggle`) and `Component Code:25` (`handleSelect`) close the dropdown via `setIsOpen(false)` but never call `buttonRef.current.focus()` or equivalent. Keyboard-a11y-tester trace `step_0005` confirms focus moves to `<body>` after Enter is pressed on an option.\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Keyboard-only users, screen reader users navigating via keyboard.\n   - **WCAG/APG Citation**: WAI-ARIA Menu Button pattern requires focus to return to the trigger button when the menu closes. WCAG 2.4.3 (Focus Order) and 2.1.2 (No Keyboard Trap).\n   - **Fix**: Store a ref to the trigger button (`const btnRef = useRef(null)`). In `handleToggle` and `handleSelect`, after closing the dropdown, call `btnRef.current?.focus()`. Ensure this happens synchronously or via `setTimeout(0)` to survive React's render cycle.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: focus-loss-on-dropdown-close\nfingerprint: a8f3c2e1d9b4\nsource: evals/results/keyboard-a11y-tester/driven/interactive-dropdown-focus-bug.trace.json step_0005\nwcag_or_apg: WAI-ARIA Menu Button pattern; WCAG 2.4.3 Focus Order\nsection_508_fpc_context: not in scope\nseverity: CRITICAL\nperspective_alarms: keyboard=HIGH, screen-reader=MEDIUM\nevidence: trace.json step_0005: keystroke Enter on listbox -> active_element_selector becomes body; focus_moved true. Component code lines 17 & 25 set isOpen(false) but lack trigger.focus().\nreproduction_steps: 1. Tab to dropdown button. 2. Press Enter to open. 3. Tab into listbox. 4. Press Enter on an option.\nexpected_behavior: Dropdown closes and focus returns to the trigger button, allowing immediate keyboard interaction with surrounding page elements.\nactual_behavior: Focus moves to <body>. User must Shift+Tab or re-Tab to find the dropdown again. Mental model of \"menu interaction\" is broken.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n2. **Roving tabindex not implemented; focus stays on listbox container.**\n   - **Evidence**: `Component Code:48` renders `<li role=\"option\">` without dynamic `tabIndex`. Only the `<ul>` has `tabIndex=\"0\"`. Arrow keys update `selectedIndex` state but do not move DOM focus to the active `<li>`.\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Screen reader users.\n   - **WCAG/APG Citation**: WAI-ARIA Listbox pattern requires roving tabindex: `tabIndex=\"0\"` on the active option, `tabIndex=\"-1\"` on others. WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value).\n   - **Fix**: Replace static `<li>` rendering with dynamic tab indexing: `tabIndex={index === selectedIndex ? 0 : -1}`. This allows screen readers to announce the option name as focus moves, and lets users navigate via Tab/Arrow interchangeably.\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n1. **Tab order into menu is accessible but non-standard.**\n   - **Evidence**: `Component Code:43` (`tabIndex=\"0\"` on `<ul>`). Users must press Tab to enter the listbox after opening it.\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Keyboard users expecting standard menu behavior.\n   - **WCAG/APG Citation**: WAI-ARIA Menu Button pattern recommends ArrowDown moving focus into the menu, not requiring a second Tab. While Tab is technically compliant, it reduces discoverability and breaks consistency with native `<select>` and common UI patterns.\n   - **Fix**: In `handleToggle`, if opening via keyboard (check `e.key === 'ArrowDown'` or `e.key === 'Enter'`), move focus to the first option (`listRef.current.children[0]?.focus()`) instead of relying on the user to Tab into it.\n\n2. **Focus appearance contrast falls below WCAG 2.4.13 threshold in some states.**\n   - **Evidence**: Keyboard-a11y-tester trace `step_0002` shows focus indicator contrast at `2.98` (fails \u22653:1). `step_0006` shows `2.53`.\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Low vision users, users with motor control difficulties.\n   - **WCAG/APG Citation**: WCAG 2.4.13 Focus Appearance (Minimum requires \u22653:1 contrast change against adjacent colors).\n   - **Fix**: Increase focus indicator outline width or color contrast to ensure the visual change meets the 3:1 threshold consistently across all interactive states.\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists):\n- `aria-haspopup=\"listbox\"` is correctly applied, but some older AT versions may not announce it. Consider adding `aria-expanded` explicitly (it's present, which is good) and ensuring the button text clearly indicates it's a dropdown (e.g., \"Sort by \u25bc\" or an `<span className=\"sr-only\">dropdown</span>`).\n\n**Enhancements** (best practice not met):\n- Add `aria-activedescendant` as an alternative to roving tabindex if DOM focus movement causes layout shifts. However, roving tabindex is generally preferred for listboxes unless the list is virtualized or extremely large.\n- Consider adding `role=\"menu\"` and `role=\"menuitem\"` instead of `listbox`/`option` if this pattern allows multiple selections or complex menu behaviors. For a single-select sort dropdown, `listbox` is correct.\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions):\n- **Missing focus restoration design**: The component assumes closing the dropdown leaves focus where it is. This is an accidental interaction model, not a designed one.\n- **Missing keyboard discoverability for menu entry**: No instruction or visual cue that Arrow keys navigate the list while the button is focused (common pattern: press Enter/ArrowDown to open, then Arrow keys navigate).\n- **Missing `aria-label` or visible text for the dropdown trigger**: The button relies on its text content (`{options[selectedIndex]}`), which changes dynamically. Screen readers will announce \"Sort by Newest\" vs \"Sort by Price\". This is actually good practice (dynamic label), but ensure the change doesn't cause excessive live region announcements if `aria-live` were added later.\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n- **Screen reader user**: Hears \"button, Sort by, not expanded\". Opens menu. Tabs into listbox, hears \"listbox, orientated vertically\". Presses ArrowDown: state updates visually, but screen reader still hears \"listbox\" because focus never moves to the option. The `aria-selected` attribute is present on the DOM node, but without roving tabindex or `aria-activedescendant`, the user cannot perceive which option is highlighted until they Tab through all of them or click.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Opens dropdown with Enter. Tabs into list. Selects an option with Enter. Focus jumps to `<body>`. User must manually navigate back to the dropdown to make another selection or move to the next form field. The interaction feels broken and disconnected from the surrounding page flow.\n- **Low vision user (200% zoom, high contrast)**: Focus indicator contrast drops below 3:1 in steps 2 and 6 of the trace. At 200% zoom, a sub-3:1 outline may become indistinguishable from the background or adjacent elements, making it hard to track focus position.\n- **Cognitive accessibility**: The mental model of \"opening a menu\" is disrupted when focus disappears to the page body upon selection. Users with cognitive load difficulties may not realize the dropdown closed or where their cursor went, leading to repeated clicks and confusion.\n\n**Verdict Justification**: \nThe component fails on two foundational interaction design decisions: focus restoration and roving tabindex. These are not aesthetic or semantic nitpicks; they are functional barriers that break keyboard operability and screen reader comprehension. The WAI-ARIA Menu Button pattern explicitly requires both. Without them, the component is 80% implemented\u2014the visible ARIA attributes are correct, but the 20% that makes it coherent for assistive technology (focus movement and restoration) is entirely missing. This warrants a REJECT verdict until focus management is redesigned and implemented.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n- Does this dropdown need to support multi-select? If so, `listbox` with `aria-multiselectable=\"true\"` changes the pattern requirements significantly.\n- Is the dropdown used inside a form submission context? If so, should the selected value be synced to a hidden `<input>` for native form submission?\n- Are there performance constraints that prevent roving tabindex (e.g., virtualized list with thousands of items)? If so, `aria-activedescendant` would be the required alternative.",
